chamber pots and mopping floors. My parents hoped I'd forget my foolish ideas and marry

25
00:02:36.897 --> 00:02:41.282
one of my suitors! But I clung stubbornly to my dream

26
00:02:41.283 --> 00:02:47.832
for seven years. In 1851 I finally enrolled in an excellent nursing

27
00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:59.564
school in Germany. After my training I took a job running a hospital for women in London. And then in 1854, Britain went to war in

28
00:02:59.565 --> 00:03:03.064
Europe. FAYE MOSS: Oh, was that

29
00:03:03.065 --> 00:03:09.453
the Crimean War? FLORENCE NIGHTINGALE: Yes. British troops went to Crimea to stop Russian

30
00:03:09.454 --> 00:03:16.478
expansion in that area. The ill or wounded soldiers were sent to a hospital across the Black Sea

31
00:03:16.479 --> 00:03:23.974
in Scutari, Turkey. Later that year I was put in charge of a team of 38 other nurses and dispatched

32
00:03:23.975 --> 00:03:27.709
to Scutari. What we saw when we arrived

33
00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:36.057
was shocking. Along with the soldiers' battle wounds, illnesses such as frostbite, dysentery, and cholera were

34
00:03:36.058 --> 00:03:47.373
rampant. Conditions inside the hospital were ghastly, with overcrowding, broken plumbing, and rats scurrying around.

35
00:03:47.374 --> 00:03:50.831
FAYE MOSS: Did you say . .

36
00:03:50.832 --> 00:03:56.962
. rats? FLORENCE NIGHTINGALE: Yes, rats, not to mention infestations of

37
00:03:56.963 --> 00:04:03.992
fleas and lice! Soldiers were sleeping on the floor without blankets, and food and medical supplies

38
00:04:03.993 --> 00:04:09.985
were scarce. During my first winter at Scutari, over four thousand

39
00:04:09.986 --> 00:04:11.925
men died.

40
00:04:11.926 --> 00:04:20.608
FAYE MOSS: How terrible. FLORENCE NIGHTINGALE: It was indeed terrible, and in 1855, the British government sent

41
00:04:20.609 --> 00:04:27.069
a sanitary commission to investigate. The commission confirmed my suspicions that the filthy conditions were

42
00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:37.676
affecting the soldiers' health. Once the hospital began to focus on sanitation—cleaning sewers, improving ventilation, and scrubbing

43
00:04:37.677 --> 00:04:45.375
everything—patients' health improved. In just a few months, the mortality rate dropped from 42.7 percent to

44
00:04:45.376 --> 00:04:48.096
2.2 percent. FAYE

45
00:04:48.097 --> 00:04:50.422
MOSS: That's impressive, Florence!

46
00:04:50.423 --> 00:04:58.646
FLORENCE NIGHTINGALE: It certainly wasn't easy. The work never ended, and many men disliked working with women—especially

47
00:04:58.647 --> 00:05:02.854
one as stubborn as me! I also traveled to the front lines to

48
00:05:02.855 --> 00:05:10.877
care for sick soldiers. During one such trip, I contracted Crimean Fever, a disease that causes

49
00:05:10.878 --> 00:05:19.327
pain and exhaustion. I was quite ill but kept working; I refused to go home until the last British patients were

50
00:05:19.328 --> 00:05:25.536
sent home. FAYE MOSS: When did you get your nickname—the Lady with the Lamp? Was it during

51
00:05:25.537 --> 00:05:32.569
the Crimean War? FLORENCE NIGHTINGALE: Yes, the soldiers called me that because I carried a lamp when I checked on them at

52
00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:39.765
night, in the darkness. The nickname gained popularity in England after a newspaper featured an illustration of me

53
00:05:39.766 --> 00:05:47.958
with my lamp. By the time I arrived home in 1856, I was quite famous—Queen Victoria even sent me a thank

54
00:05:47.959 --> 00:05:55.561
you note! However, fame didn't interest me one bit. I just wanted to be left alone to continue

55
00:05:55.562 --> 00:06:00.176
working. FAYE MOSS: How was your health? Were you able to continue with

56
00:06:00.177 --> 00:06:08.263
your nursing career? FLORENCE NIGHTINGALE: No, unfortunately, the Crimean Fever had left me

57
00:06:08.264 --> 00:06:13.865
in almost constant pain. But I found a way to use my knowledge of statistics to

58
00:06:13.866 --> 00:06:25.105
improve medical care. I'd taken extensive notes during the war, and a colleague and I began analyzing data about military hospitals for a report on the health of the British

59
00:06:25.106 --> 00:06:31.014
army. The report, released in 1858, was over five hundred pages

60
00:06:31.015 --> 00:06:42.036
long. It caused quite a stir because I was one of the first researchers to use diagrams in addition to statistics to illustrate important trends in medical care. FAYE

61
00:06:42.037 --> 00:06:44.823
MOSS: Did it lead to changes in

62
00:06:44.824 --> 00:06:54.509
healthcare? FLORENCE NIGHTINGALE: It most certainly did. From my data, lawmakers could now clearly see, for example, that sanitation was

63
00:06:54.510 --> 00:07:00.137
hugely important; more British soldiers died from preventable causes than from battle

64
00:07:00.138 --> 00:07:05.880
wounds! My work led to cleaner hospitals and even a new sewer system in London.

65
00:07:05.881 --> 00:07:12.496
I also became the first female fellow in the Royal Statistical Society—quite an honor.

66
00:07:12.497 --> 00:07:16.866
FAYE MOSS: I'll say! Didn't you establish a nursing school

67
00:07:16.867 --> 00:07:22.810
too? FLORENCE NIGHTINGALE: Yes, the Nightingale School of Nursing opened in

68
00:07:22.811 --> 00:07:31.722
London in 1860. We trained nurses and instituted practices that became standard, such as frequent hand-washing and

69
00:07:31.723 --> 00:07:43.161
wearing uniforms. Each year I sent a letter to the students, gently reminding them to maintain important nursing principles such as kindness, sympathy, and trustworthiness.

70
00:07:43.162 --> 00:07:54.784
I also continued researching aspects of medical care, including ways to improve working and living conditions for the poor and recommendations for hospital design. FAYE

71
00:07:54.785 --> 00:08:02.015
MOSS: Wow! At a time when most women didn't have careers, you revolutionized the field of nursing!

72
00:08:02.016 --> 00:08:09.799
And you fulfilled your personal ambition to reduce suffering in the world. We'll let you return to history now. FLORENCE

73
00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:14.750
NIGHTINGALE:

74
00:08:14.751 --> 00:08:24.153
Good-bye! FAYE MOSS: Florence Nightingale was a talented nurse and a remarkable statistician whose research changed

75
00:08:24.154 --> 00:08:32.559
healthcare forever. She died in 1910. Her accomplishments also inspired many other women to become

76
00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:39.662
